[272.016691, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\n# Vulnerability Report: Entry Vector Analysis for `rdp-prod-01`  \n\n## 1. Executive Vulnerability Summary\n- **Primary Entry Vector**: Password Brute Force (`T1110`) against RDP (`T1021.001`) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`)\n- **Software Vulnerability (CVE) Involvement**: **NONE / Explicit Absence** (`/sas/vulns.json`)\n- **Confidence Level**: **HIGH**\n\n---\n\n## 2. Reconciling CVE Claims (CVE-2019-0708 / BlueKeep Evaluation)\n\nThe initial remediation draft ticket (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`) proposed patching **CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)**. \n\nForensic analysis and threat intelligence confirmation demonstrate that **CVE-2019-0708 was NOT exploited** during this incident:\n1. **Host OS Version**: The system is running **Windows Server 2022** (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE`). BlueKeep (CVE-2019-0708) affects Windows 7, Windows Server 2008 R2, and Windows Server 2008. Windows Server 2022 is not vulnerable to BlueKeep.\n2. **Vulnerability Feed**: The vulnerability scan report at `/sas/vulns.json` contains an empty list `[]`, indicating no software vulnerabilities were flagged by the security product.\n3. **Threat Intelligence Mapping**: The threat advisory (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`) attributes the initial entry step explicitly to **T1110 (Brute Force)** via RDP logon, rather than remote code execution exploit payloads.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Configuration Exposure vs. Software Vulnerability\n\nRather than exploiting a software CVE, the attacker took advantage of weak identity and remote access baseline controls:\n- **NLA Disabled**: Remote Desktop Protocol configuration (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`) had `UserAuthentication = 0`, allowing unauthenticated RDP connection handshakes.\n- **Exposed RDP Port**: TCP port 3389 was reachable directly from external IP `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n- **Credential Hygiene**: Weak or guessable password policy enabled successful brute-forcing of administrative credentials (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Conclusion & Recommendations\nNo CVE remediation is required for initial access prevention on this host. Remediation efforts must focus on network access control, password policy enforcement, and enabling NLA on RDP (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\nEOF\n"]
[272.016912, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/vuln-report.md\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[272.017343, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> # Vulnerability Report: Entry Vector Analysis for `rdp-prod-01`  \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## 1. Executive Vulnerability Summary\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[272.019083, "o", "- **Primary Entry Vector**: Password Brute Force (`T1110`) against RDP (`T1021.001`) (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> - **Software Vulnerability (CVE) Involvement**: **NONE / Explicit Absence** (`/sas/vulns.json`)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> - **Confidence Level**: **HIGH**\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ---\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## 2. Reconciling CVE Claims (CVE-2019-0708 / BlueKeep Evaluation)\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> The initial remediation draft ticket (`/sas/handoff/change-ticket.md:10`) proposed patching **CVE-2019-0708 (BlueKeep)**. \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Forensic analysis and threat intelligence confirmation demonstrate that **CVE-2019-0708 was NOT exploited** during this incident:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 1. **Host OS Version**: The system is running **Windows Server 2022** (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE`). BlueKeep (CVE-2019-0708) affects Windows 7, Windows Server 2008 R2, and Windows Server 2008. Windows Server 2022 is not vulnerable to BlueKeep.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 2. **Vulnerability Feed**: The vulnerability scan report at `/sas/vulns.json` contains an empty list `[]`, indicating no software vulnerabilities were flagged by the security product.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> 3. **Threat Intelligence Mapping**: The threat advisory (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`) attributes the initial entry step explicitly to **T1110 (Brute Force)** via RDP logon, rather than remote code execution exploit payloads.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ---\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ## 3. Configuration Exposure vs. Software Vulnerability\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> Rather than exploiting a software CVE, the attacker took advantage of weak identity and remote access baseline controls:\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[272.019201, "o", "> "]
[272.019467, "o", "- **NLA Disabled**: Remote Desktop Protocol configuration (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`) had `UserAuthentication = 0`, allowing unauthenticated RDP connection handshakes.\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[272.019701, "o", "\u001b[?2004h"]
[272.019805, "o", "> "]
[272.020438, "o", "- **Exposed RDP Port**: TCP port 3389 was reachable directly from external IP `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[272.020726, "o", "- **Credential Hygiene**: Weak or guessable password policy enabled successful brute-forcing of administrative credentials (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> \r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> ---\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[272.020745, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h"]
[272.020826, "o", "> "]
[272.021053, "o", "## 4. Conclusion & Recommendations"]
[272.021195, "o", "\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> "]
[272.021627, "o", "No CVE remediation is required for initial access prevention on this host. Remediation efforts must focus on network access control, password policy enforcement, and enabling NLA on RDP (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r\u001b[?2004h> EOF\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[272.0227, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@95313cc97448:/app# "]
[285.392083, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /app/baseline-report.md\n# Configuration Baseline Weaknesses Report: `rdp-prod-01`  \n\n## 1. Executive Summary\nAn evaluation of configuration baselines on host `rdp-prod-01` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/SOFTWARE`) revealed multiple critical baseline security gaps that facilitated initial intrusion, persistence, and anti-forensics. The primary weakness is an insecure Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) baseline flagged in check `CHK-06` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Identified Baseline Weaknesses & Evidence\n\n### 1. RDP Network Level Authentication (NLA) Disabled (`CHK-06` / `BAS-01`)\n- **Finding**: RDP configuration file `/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg` explicitly records `UserAuthentication = 0 (NLA disabled), connections allowed` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\n- **Telemetry Reference**: Flagged in SAS baseline telemetry check `CHK-06` (`/sas/baseline.json:3`, `/sas/alerts.json:26`).\n- **Impact**: Disabling NLA allows unauthenticated clients to establish RDP sessions and perform brute-force attacks against the Windows logon interface without authenticating at the network layer first.\n\n### 2. Direct Internet Exposure of Management Services\n- **Finding**: RDP (TCP port 3389) was directly exposed to public IP addresses including threat origin IP `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n- **Impact**: Unrestricted exposure of remote administration interfaces invites automated password spraying and brute-forcing (`T1110`).\n\n### 3. Account Security & Password Policy Weakness\n- **Finding**: Administrative accounts were susceptible to automated credential brute forcing (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:11`).\n- **Impact**: Absence of strict account lockout thresholds permitted sustained credential attacks without locking out compromised accounts.\n\n### 4. Event Logging & Audit Policy Deficiencies\n- **Finding**: The Security Event log was cleared via `EventID 1102` (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx`).\n- **Impact**: Lack of centralized, off-host log shipping allowed the attacker to destroy local security log evidence (`T1070.001`) without leaving an off-site trace.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recommended Baseline Hardening Rules\n1. **Enable Network Level Authentication (NLA)**: Set `UserAuthentication = 1` under `HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\WinStations\\RDP-Tcp` (`/disk/Windows/System32/config/RDP-Tcp.reg:4`).\n2. **Restrict Remote Access**: Place RDP behind a VPN / zero-trust network access gateway; block inbound traffic from unknown external IPs like `198.51.100.92` (`/sas/cti/advisory.txt:5`).\n3. **Enforce Account Lockout & Password Complexity**: Configure Group Policy to lock accounts after 5 failed attempts.\n4. **Implement Centralized Security Logging**: Ship Windows Event Logs (`Security.evtx`, `System.evtx`) to a SIEM in real-time (`/disk/Windows/System32/winevt/Logs/Security.evtx`).\nEOF\n"]
